{"kind":"access","id":"R0014","status":"READ","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":4,"section":"III. SYSTEM OVERVIEW","quote":"The goal of ASAP is to conduct efficient and reliable collaborative computing in the UAV relay swarm, which relieves the burden of air-ground communication by transmitting valuable data results instead of raw data.","reason":null}
{"kind":"access","id":"R0036","status":"READ","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":4,"section":"III. SYSTEM DESCRIPTION AND PROBLEM FORMULATION","quote":"Given most UAVs' limited compute, video analytics offload occurs at the ground-based edge server: UAVs keep encoding and transmitting videos to the server where a pre-trained object detector (e.g., Yolo [36]) for a specific purpose is launched, and the videos are fed into the model frame by frame for inference.","reason":null}
{"kind":"access","id":"R0038","status":"READ","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0038.pdf","page":3,"section":"III.B System Model","quote":"We split the CNN architecture into four logical components. The split is done across the convolutional layers present in the CNN to provide a balanced process for each UAV device in the network.","reason":null}
{"kind":"access","id":"R0043","status":"READ","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0043.pdf","page":1,"section":"I. INTRODUCTION","quote":"Our solution is not to transmit the collected video data to the cloud, but to offload the computing task to the dedicated computing UAVs equipped with server.","reason":null}
{"kind":"access","id":"R0058","status":"READ","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0058.pdf","page":5,"section":"3.1. Inference offloading model","quote":"The system consists of a set of autonomous UAVs N = {1,2,...,N} and edge servers M = {1,2,...,M} deployed at ground base stations.","reason":null}
{"kind":"access","id":"R0064","status":"READ","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0064.pdf","page":4,"section":"3. System model and problem formulation","quote":"Specifically, UAVs first fly to PoIs and take a video with a certain number of frames and a fixed high resolution. Then, UAVs perform the computation offloading decision to determine whether the DNN inference task is executed locally or offloaded to any one of the ESs.","reason":null}
{"kind":"access","id":"R0075","status":"READ","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0075.pdf","page":4,"section":"III.A Task Definition","quote":"We consider a multi-UAV collaborative inference system consisting of one Master UAV and multiple Assistant UAVs. Given an input sample x, each UAV is equipped with a lightweight multi-exit student model and can extract intermediate representations at different network depths.","reason":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0014","key":"architecture","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Hierarchical clustered UAV swarm (task UAVs and relay UAVs organized into clusters with cluster heads and cluster members) performing autonomous airborne collaborative DL inference without relying on base stations or ground stations; submodels execute in a pipeline-parallel manner across clusters and data-parallel within each cluster.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":1,"section":"Abstract","quote":"exploiting the resources of the UAV swarm including both task UAVs and relay UAVs, we build up an all-sky autonomous computing (ASAP) system"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":4,"section":"III. SYSTEM OVERVIEW","quote":"First, the complete DL model is partitioned into three submodels for each cluster"}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0014","key":"workload","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Video-frame inference across 3 medium CNNs (VGG16, ResNet34, DarkNet19) and 3 large ResNet models (ResNet50, ResNet101, ResNet152) at three video resolutions: high (720x1280), medium (540x960), and low (180x320); plus pre-trained VGG16 on CIFAR-10 for intermediate data compression evaluation.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":11,"section":"VIII.A Evaluation Setup","quote":"We evaluate the performance of ASAP with 3 medium DL models and 3 large DL models, which are classified according to the model size."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":14,"section":"VIII.E Performance of Adaptive Inter-UAV Data Compressor","quote":"We conduct the experiment with a pre-trained VGG16 model, which is trained on the CIFAR-10 dataset."}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0014","key":"partition_assignment","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Two-tier partitioning: layer-wise model partitioning across UAV clusters via External Cluster Load Balancing (ECLB) and spatial input-range data partitioning within each cluster via Mapping Range Transforming (MRT) and Internal Cluster Load Balancing (ICLB), paired with customized per-layer zero padding and adaptive 8-bit quantization plus gzip compression on inter-UAV intermediate tensors.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":5,"section":"III. SYSTEM OVERVIEW","quote":"the proposed architecture partitions the DL model and data among UAV clusters and within each cluster, respectively"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":6,"section":"IV.A Inter-Cluster Load Balancing","quote":"design the Internal Cluster Load Balancing (ICLB) algorithm to generate data partition strategy in the cluster."}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0014","key":"optimization","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Decision variables: submodel layer sets M_c per cluster and input/output spatial ranges R_in^m/R_out^m per UAV. Objective: minimize collaborative bottleneck latency max{l_1,l_2,...,l_N} by equalizing predicted latency across clusters and intra-cluster nodes. Method: greedy iterative load-balancing heuristics (MRT, ICLB, ECLB, Elastic Scheduling ES) guided by a two-stage latency predictor (offline operator-level staircase/linear model + 16-neuron hidden-layer TensorRT operator-fusion fine-tuner).","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":6,"section":"IV.A Inter-Cluster Load Balancing","quote":"the collaborative latency of a task in a cluster is max{l_1,l_2,...,l_N}."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":8,"section":"V.B Latency Fusion Fine-Tuning","quote":"we use a very small model, with only 16 neurons in the hidden layer, to learn the fusion rules of deep learning framework runtime"}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0014","key":"compute_radio","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Compute: 24 airborne computers (20 NVIDIA Jetson Nano [~7.6 GFLOPS FP32, 4 GB RAM on p1], 2 Jetson Xavier TX2, 2 Jetson Xavier NX), 5 real-world quad-rotor UAVs (4 Jetson Nano, 1 Jetson NX), and 1 RTX 3060 GPU ground station for offloading baselines. Radio: WiFi router for the 24-node indoor testbed, Ad-hoc wireless modules for the 5 real-world UAVs, SSDP multicast discovery (239.255.255.250:1900), and EWMA communication rate estimation (a=0.2).","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":10,"section":"VIII.A Evaluation Setup","quote":"We have deployed ASAP on 24 UAV airborne computers, including 20 Jetson Nano, 2 Jetson Xavier TX2, and 2 Jetson Xavier NX"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":10,"section":"VIII.A Evaluation Setup","quote":"deployed on 5 real-world UAVs, which are equipped with 4 Jetson Nano and 1 Jetson NX, and the UAVs transmit data through the Ad hoc modules on UAVs"}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0014","key":"mobility_continuity","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Node join/leave and link instability handled via SSDP discovery, 2-second periodic alive announcements with disconnection timers, Elastic Scheduling (ES) to recompute ECLB/ICLB upon cluster-head or member loss/recovery, and adaptive 8-bit quantization scaling with data-size-to-rate ratio. Tested under intentional cluster-head disconnection and recovery over a 1,000-task sequence.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":10,"section":"VII.B Implementation Tricks","quote":"For each cluster member, a live announcement message is sent to the cluster head periodically with a dedicated port to show the connection status of the UAV."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":13,"section":"VIII.C Performance of Elastic Efficient Scheduler","quote":"in which the communication of one cluster head is cut off during a collaborative computing task."}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0014","key":"evaluation","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Physical hardware testbeds (>4,000 lines of Python): 24 Jetson airborne computers over WiFi (including an 11-node heterogeneous subset) and 5 real-world quad-rotor UAVs over Ad-hoc links, plus TensorRT latency profiling datasets (6,000 training / 2,000 test operator samples).","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":9,"section":"VII. IMPLEMENTATION","quote":"The self-developed system consists of over 4,000 lines of Python code"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":10,"section":"VIII.A Evaluation Setup","quote":"the devices communicate with each other through a WiFi network, as shown in Fig. 12(a)."}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0014","key":"baselines","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Raw data offloading to RTX 3060 ground station (20 m, 50 m, 100 m heights), On-board single-UAV execution, Neurosurgeon (edge-device split at 20 m, 50 m, 100 m), DeepSlicing (4 submodels), MoDNN (VGG16/DarkNet19 only), Average Scheduling, FLOPs-based linear latency predictor, and nn-Meter.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":11,"section":"VIII.A Evaluation Setup","quote":"We compare the performance of ASAP against three kinds of works."}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0014","key":"outcomes","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Reports average inference latency (s), per-node compute (GFLOPs) and memory (MB) overhead, rescheduling latency (415.58-989.30 ms), predictor accuracy/latency (12.3-28.0 ms vs 9057.6-292326.3 ms for nn-Meter), and data-size reduction (87.2%-92.7% with <0.15% accuracy drop). On ResNet50 (720x1280, N=100), ASAP achieves 0.75 s at 12 nodes vs 1.72 s On-board, 5.26-5.69 s Raw Data Offloading, and 6.09-8.63 s Neurosurgeon.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":12,"section":"VIII.B Overall Performance","quote":"Table III compares the average inference latency of ASAP with two types of data offloading works."}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0014","key":"variability","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Evaluates node-count scaling (4-20 nodes), 3 video resolutions, heterogeneous vs homogeneous clusters, 3 offloading heights (20-100 m), quantization scale sweep (0.1-0.6), and a 1,000-task failure/recovery trace; multi-seed dispersion (SD/CI/IQR) is not reported.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":11,"section":"VIII.B Overall Performance","quote":"the average inference latency with the collaboration of a different number of UAV airborne computers (Jetson Nano) is measured, ranging from 4 to 20."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":14,"section":"VIII.E Performance of Adaptive Inter-UAV Data Compressor","quote":"For quantization scales from 0.1 to 0.6, the intermediate data size can be reduced by 87.2%~92.7%"}],"gap":"Multi-run variance/confidence intervals are not reported in Section VIII."}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0014","key":"code_data","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Author source code is open-sourced at https://github.com/snhao222/ASAP (>4,000 lines of Python); CIFAR-10 is used for quantization accuracy evaluation, while the source video used for latency benchmarking is not named or linked.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":3,"section":"I. INTRODUCTION","quote":"To benefit the community, ASAP is open sourced at https://github.com/snhao222/ASAP."}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0014","key":"funding_conflicts","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Funding is reported (NSFC Grants 61931011 and 62072303); conflicts of interest are not reported.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":1,"section":"First-page footnote","quote":"This work was supported in part by the National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant 61931011 and Grant 62072303."}],"gap":"No conflict-of-interest disclosure found on p1 or pp15-16."}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0014","key":"limitations","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Authors note ASAP focuses on single-task inference flows (not RL training or multi-task-flow scheduling), lacks attention-head scheduling for transformers, experiences latency rebound when too many nodes are added, and requires further study on optimal UAV counts and volatile-network fault tolerance.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":15,"section":"IX. DISCUSSION","quote":"considering that the proposed system is mainly focused on the inference process, it may be hard to apply ASAP to the training of RL."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":15,"section":"IX. DISCUSSION","quote":"The proposed system mainly considers the situation of a single task flow"}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"quantity","id":"R0014","key":"outcomes","value":"0.75 vs 1.72 (On-board), 5.26 (Raw Data Offloading 20m), 6.09 (Neurosurgeon 20m)","units":"s (average inference latency per task)","comparator":"On-board, Raw Data Offloading (20m), Neurosurgeon (20m)","workload":"ResNet50 on high-resolution (720x1280) video, N=100 tasks","conditions":"ASAP with 12 Jetson Nano nodes over WiFi vs single Jetson Nano on-board and offloading to RTX 3060 ground station at 20m height","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","page":12,"section":"VIII.B Overall Performance","table_or_figure":"TABLE III","quote":"ResNet50 | 1.72 | 5.26 | 6.09 | 0.75"}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0036","key":"architecture","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Star/hub-and-spoke multi-UAV-to-edge-server architecture: N low-altitude camera-equipped UAVs monitor potentially overlapping 2D grid cells and upload pre-processed video chunks to a nearby ground edge server, which hosts the object detector and a centralized configuration controller.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":4,"section":"III. SYSTEM DESCRIPTION AND PROBLEM FORMULATION","quote":"There is a set of N low-altitude UAVs, denoted by S = {s_1, s_2, ..., s_n, ..., s_N}, distributed in G and connected to an edge server nearby."}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0036","key":"workload","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Real-time pedestrian object detection using YOLOv5 on the ETH pedestrian detection dataset (1,804 images across 3 video clips, 640x480, 14 FPS, split into 10-second chunks), with Lucas-Kanade optical flow for target velocity and bounding-box size for target scale profiling.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":10,"section":"VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION","quote":"We use the widely-adopted "ETH" pedestrian detection dataset [40] for video analytics, which contains 1,804 images in three video clips captured from a stereo rig mounted on car, with a resolution of 640×480 and a frame rate of 14 FPS."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":10,"section":"VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION","quote":"The edge server runs a YOLOv5 object detection model to generate bounding boxes of objects for each offloaded frame."}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0036","key":"partition_assignment","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Full-frame video-chunk offloading without DNN layer splitting: UAVs perform front-end frame sampling (f_n^t) and resolution resizing (r_n^t) per 10-s chunk, while the ground edge server executes YOLOv5 inference and runs the SAC^2 accuracy profiler and UAV/configuration selector.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":4,"section":"III. SYSTEM DESCRIPTION AND PROBLEM FORMULATION","quote":"The raw surveillance video is chunk-level pre-processed at the front-end UAV by sampling frames with certain frame rate and resizing frames with a suitable resolution"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":4,"section":"III. SYSTEM DESCRIPTION AND PROBLEM FORMULATION","quote":"the controller at the edge server decides for each UAV whether the following video chunk is necessary for edge inference and which configuration should be used"}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0036","key":"optimization","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Decision variables: binary UAV selection x_n^t, integer resolution r_n^t, integer frame rate f_n^t (reformulated as binary alpha_{r,f}^n). Objective: maximize weighted effective surveillance coverage (monotone submodular). Constraints: per-frame delay <= T_th, chunk F1-based accuracy >= phi_th, total frame rate <= C (knapsack), total bandwidth <= B (knapsack), and <=1 configuration per UAV (partition matroid). Solver: pre-trained NN accuracy profiler + 2D binary-search feasible-set filtering + frame-rate enumeration into l subproblems solved by greedy 2-swap local search with (1-e^-3)/3 approximation ratio.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":5,"section":"III.C Problem Formulation","quote":"we can formulate the joint UAV selection and configuration control problem for any time slot t (∀t ∈ T) into an integer programming problem"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":9,"section":"V.C Configuration Control Optimizer","quote":"we apply a greedy local search based algorithm to find a solution to the submodularity maximization with theoretical approximation guarantee."}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0036","key":"compute_radio","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Radio modeled via Shannon capacity R_n^t = b_n^t log_2(1 + p_n^t |h_n^t|^2 / N_0) with shared uplink bandwidth B (swept 1-20 Mbps); edge compute modeled as total object-detection processing frequency C (swept 10-100 FPS) with fixed per-frame DNN time T^dnn. Physical hardware CPU/GPU specs and onboard UAV compute specs are not reported.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":5,"section":"III.B System Description","quote":"where the data rate is given by R_n^t = b_n^t log_2(1 + p_n^t |h_n^t|^2 / N_0)."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":12,"section":"VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION","quote":"evaluated under varying available network bandwidth, edge processing capacity, and number of UAVs"}],"gap":"Specific physical compute hardware/chipset models are not reported."}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0036","key":"mobility_continuity","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Models dynamic scene content (variable target sizes and optical-flow motion velocities), variable UAV flight altitude/FoV, and varying bandwidth/compute capacity; explicit UAV flight trajectories, intermittent link outages, UAV failures, and recovery mechanisms are not modeled or evaluated.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":4,"section":"III. SYSTEM DESCRIPTION AND PROBLEM FORMULATION","quote":"each UAV with built-in camera is capable of capturing dynamic scene views within its variable flight altitude and camera field of view"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":11,"section":"VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION","quote":"delves into the impacts of video content dynamics, including the averaged target size and target velocity"}],"gap":"No explicit UAV mobility trajectories, link-outage events, or node-failure recovery evaluated (inspected pp4-12)."}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0036","key":"evaluation","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Trace-driven simulation using the ETH pedestrian dataset (1,804 images, 10-s chunks, 900-grid surveillance area, 100 randomly distributed UAVs with 3-grid radius, YOLOv5 object detector); simulator software and host hardware are not reported.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":10,"section":"VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION","quote":"We evaluate the performance of our SAC^2 scheme via extensive simulations."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":11,"section":"VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION","quote":"We set 100 UAVs randomly distributed in the targeted surveillance area"}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0036","key":"baselines","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Fixed Configuration (FC), Capacity Based Adaptation (CBA), All-to-upload (AU), Random-to-upload (RU, 20 trials), and exhaustive-search Optimal (OPT).","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":10,"section":"VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION","quote":"we compare the performance of our SAC^2 against several baselines, i.e.:"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":12,"section":"VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION","quote":"OPT represents the optimal strategy derived by searching out the feasible set exhaustively"}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0036","key":"outcomes","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Reports accumulated effective surveillance coverage, per-frame F1-based detection accuracy, and Lucas-Kanade optical-flow motion estimation. SAC^2 improves accumulated surveillance coverage by 22% over RU at 20 Mbps bandwidth and by up to 65% for small-sized, high-speed-moving targets, while keeping accuracy near ~0.68-0.70 where FC degrades to ~0.45.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":12,"section":"VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION","quote":"SAC^2 achieves a notable 22% improvement in accumulated surveillance coverage over RU at a communication bandwidth of 20Mbps."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":12,"section":"VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION","quote":"When video frames contain small-sized and high-speed-moving targets, SAC^2 can enhance coverage by as much as 65%."}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0036","key":"variability","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Sweeps bandwidth (1-20 Mbps), edge compute capacity (10-100 FPS), UAV count (10-100), and 9 video-content difficulty categories; RU is averaged over 20 trials, but SD/CI/IQR error bars are not reported on the plots.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":12,"section":"VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION","quote":"For RU, we repeat 20 trials on each parameter setting and record the mean value of the accumulated effective coverage."}],"gap":"No SD, CI, or error bars reported in Figs. 6-8."}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0036","key":"code_data","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Uses the public ETH pedestrian detection dataset [40]; no author code or trained-model repository is provided.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":10,"section":"VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION","quote":"We use the widely-adopted "ETH" pedestrian detection dataset [40] for video analytics"}],"gap":"No author code repository URL provided (inspected pp1-13)."}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0036","key":"funding_conflicts","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Funding is reported (NSFC 62201071, 62201311, 62471056, 62501072; PCL PCL2025A08, 2025QYB041, 2025QYA002; Ministry of Emergency Management E08-01); conflicts of interest are not reported.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":1,"section":"First-page footnote","quote":"The work of Liang Li and Wen Wu was supported in part by National Natural Science Foundation of China under grants 62201071 and 62201311"}],"gap":"No conflict-of-interest disclosure found on p1 or pp13-16."}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0036","key":"limitations","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Authors state future work is needed to capture spatiotemporal correlations across UAV video frames for lightweight online adaptation and to exploit 3D aerial scene dynamics; evaluation also relies on ground-view car-mounted ETH video clips rather than aerial UAV footage.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":13,"section":"VII. CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK","quote":"Future work will explore capturing spatiotemporal correlations across UAV video frames for lightweight online configuration adaptation and extend SAC^2 to exploit 3D scene dynamics unique to aerial perspectives."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":10,"section":"VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION","quote":"captured from a stereo rig mounted on car"}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"quantity","id":"R0036","key":"outcomes","value":"+22%","units":"% relative improvement in accumulated effective surveillance coverage","comparator":"Random-to-upload (RU, mean of 20 trials)","workload":"ETH pedestrian dataset (1,804 frames, 640x480, 14 FPS, 10-s chunks) with YOLOv5 object detector","conditions":"100 UAVs across 900 grids (3-grid radius), 20 Mbps communication bandwidth","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":12,"section":"VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION","table_or_figure":"Fig. 8(a) / text","quote":"SAC^2 achieves a notable 22% improvement in accumulated surveillance coverage over RU at a communication bandwidth of 20Mbps."}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0038","key":"architecture","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Pipeline-parallel 4-stage distributed CNN orchestrated by Docker Swarm with a 3-tier Redis key/value data store (Redis-master on Ubuntu Desktop base station, Replica-1/Sentinel-1 on Ubuntu Laptop replica base station, Replica-2/Sentinel-2 and Sentinel-3 on two Raspberry Pi 5 UAVs) communicating over a Docker Swarm DNS overlay network.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0038.pdf","page":3,"section":"III.B System Model","quote":"We split the CNN architecture into four logical components."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0038.pdf","page":5,"section":"V.A Experimental Setup","quote":"deployed on an Ubuntu desktop represent-ing the base station of the UAVs, and an Ubuntu-Linux laptop representing a replica base station node for the Redis Sentinel. These nodes mirrored a replica Sentinel in a pair of UAVs running on Raspberry Pis"}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0038","key":"workload","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Aircraft image classification on 100 images (255x255 pixels) using a 4-stage TensorFlow CNN (Input/Conv+Maxpool, Conv Layer 1+Maxpool, Conv Layer 2+Maxpool, Flattening+Dense+Output); specific dataset name not reported.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0038.pdf","page":5,"section":"V.A Experimental Setup","quote":"The experiments involved classifying one hundred images with a standard resolution of 255×255 pixels to simulate CNN inference workloads."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0038.pdf","page":5,"section":"V.B.1 Inference Time","quote":"accuracy of the CNN to correctly identify the aircraft in the image"}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0038","key":"partition_assignment","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Layer-wise CNN fragmentation into 4 discrete Docker containers (Input layer, Conv Layer 1, Conv Layer 2, Output layer) synchronized via Redis checkpointing; Docker Swarm distributes CNN containers and enforces YAML placement constraints for Redis Master/Sentinels.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0038.pdf","page":3,"section":"IV. SYSTEM IMPLEMENTATION","quote":"Using Docker Swarm, the CNN model split by layer is distributed to available devices for the inference process."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0038.pdf","page":4,"section":"IV.C Orchestrating CNN Layers with Docker Swarm","quote":"Each stage of the CNN model was separated into its own discrete Docker container."}],"gap":null}
{"kind":"field","id":"R0038","key":"optimization","status":"NOT_REPORTED","finding":"No mathematical optimization formulation, objective function, or scheduling solver is reported; container placement and failover rely on Docker Swarm YAML placement constraints and restart policies.","locators":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0038.pdf","page":4,"section":"IV.C Orchestrating CNN Layers with Docker Swarm","quote":null}],"gap":"No optimization variables, objective, constraints, or solver reported (inspected pp3-4)."}
